  "summary": "A chemical dosing system injects a measured quantity of chemical (coagulant, chlorine, inhibitor, pH control, odorant) into a process or water line. Chemical is stored in a tank (often with a mixer), drawn through a strainer by a duty metering pump (with a standby), and delivered through a pulsation dampener and back-pressure valve to an injection quill. A calibration column on the suction lets operators measure the actual pump output; a safety relief valve protects the pump and piping; everything sits on a bunded skid to contain spills.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Package / system (controlled-volume pumps)",
    "configuration": "Duty/standby diaphragm metering pumps on bunded skid",
    "application": "Water treatment, process chemical injection"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "pumps": "2 × diaphragm metering pumps (duty/standby)",
    "accessories": "Strainer, calibration column, pulsation dampener, PSV, back-pressure valve, gauge, injection quill",
    "materials_note": "Wetted materials (PP, PVDF, PTFE, 316 SS, Alloy 20) must suit the chemical; check compatibility tables."
  },
